Week 12 — Simplified Lab Procedure
Step 1: Create the project folder
Open the Ubuntu terminal and execute:
docker-compose down
mkdir Week-12
cd Week-12


If you already have a Week-12 folder, use cd Week-12 instead of creating it again.
Step 2: Create the Hadoop configuration file
nano hadoop.env


Paste the following:
CORE_CONF_fs_defaultFS=hdfs://namenode:9000
CORE_CONF_hadoop_http_staticuser_user=root
CORE_CONF_io_compression_codecs=org.apache.hadoop.io.compress.SnappyCodec
HDFS_CONF_dfs_webhdfs_enabled=true
HDFS_CONF_dfs_permissions_enabled=false
HDFS_CONF_dfs_namenode_datanode_registration_ip___hostname___check=false


Save the file: press Ctrl + O, Enter, then Ctrl + X.
Step 3: Create the Docker Compose file
nano docker-compose.yml


Paste the complete, simplified configuration below. It includes ZooKeeper, Kafka, NameNode, DataNode, and Kafka Connect.

services:
  zookeeper:
    image: confluentinc/cp-zookeeper:7.4.0
    hostname: zookeeper
    container_name: zookeeper
    ports:
      - "2181:2181"
    environment:
      ZOOKEEPER_CLIENT_PORT: 2181
      ZOOKEEPER_TICK_TIME: 2000

  kafka:
    image: confluentinc/cp-kafka:7.4.0
    hostname: kafka
    container_name: kafka
    depends_on:
      - zookeeper
    ports:
      - "9093:9093"
    environment:
      KAFKA_BROKER_ID: 1
      KAFKA_ZOOKEEPER_CONNECT: zookeeper:2181
      KAFKA_LISTENER_SECURITY_PROTOCOL_MAP: PLAINTEXT:PLAINTEXT,PLAINTEXT_HOST:PLAINTEXT
      KAFKA_LISTENERS: PLAINTEXT://0.0.0.0:9092,PLAINTEXT_HOST://0.0.0.0:9093
      KAFKA_ADVERTISED_LISTENERS: PLAINTEXT://kafka:9092,PLAINTEXT_HOST://localhost:9093
      KAFKA_OFFSETS_TOPIC_REPLICATION_FACTOR: 1
      KAFKA_TRANSACTION_STATE_LOG_REPLICATION_FACTOR: 1
      KAFKA_TRANSACTION_STATE_LOG_MIN_ISR: 1

  namenode:
    image: bde2020/hadoop-namenode:2.0.0-hadoop3.2.1-java8
    container_name: namenode
    environment:
      CLUSTER_NAME: lab-cluster
    env_file:
      - ./hadoop.env
    ports:
      - "9870:9870"
      - "9000:9000"
    volumes:
      - namenode-data:/hadoop/dfs/name

  datanode:
    image: bde2020/hadoop-datanode:2.0.0-hadoop3.2.1-java8
    container_name: datanode
    depends_on:
      - namenode
    environment:
      SERVICE_PRECONDITION: "namenode:9870"
    env_file:
      - ./hadoop.env
    volumes:
      - datanode-data:/hadoop/dfs/data

  kafka-connect:
    image: confluentinc/cp-kafka-connect:7.4.0
    hostname: kafka-connect
    container_name: kafka-connect
    depends_on:
      - kafka
      - namenode
      - datanode
    ports:
      - "8083:8083"
    environment:
      CONNECT_BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS: kafka:9092
      CONNECT_REST_ADVERTISED_HOST_NAME: kafka-connect
      CONNECT_REST_PORT: 8083
      CONNECT_GROUP_ID: connect-cluster
      CONNECT_CONFIG_STORAGE_TOPIC: connect-configs
      CONNECT_OFFSET_STORAGE_TOPIC: connect-offsets
      CONNECT_STATUS_STORAGE_TOPIC: connect-status
      CONNECT_CONFIG_STORAGE_REPLICATION_FACTOR: 1
      CONNECT_OFFSET_STORAGE_REPLICATION_FACTOR: 1
      CONNECT_STATUS_STORAGE_REPLICATION_FACTOR: 1
      CONNECT_KEY_CONVERTER: org.apache.kafka.connect.storage.StringConverter
      CONNECT_VALUE_CONVERTER: org.apache.kafka.connect.storage.StringConverter
      CONNECT_PLUGIN_PATH: /usr/share/java,/usr/share/confluent-hub-components
    command:
      - bash
      - -c
      - |
        confluent-hub install --no-prompt confluentinc/kafka-connect-hdfs:latest
        /etc/confluent/docker/run

volumes:
  namenode-data:
  datanode-data:



Save with Ctrl + O → Enter → Ctrl + X.
Step 4: Start all containers
docker compose up -d


Wait for the containers to start, then run:
docker ps


You should see five containers:
- zookeeper
- kafka
- namenode
- datanode
- kafka-connect
If your Ubuntu system uses the older Compose command, use docker-compose instead of docker compose.
Step 5: Check that HDFS is working
docker exec namenode hdfs dfsadmin -report


Look for:
Live datanodes (1)


You can also open the Hadoop NameNode page in your browser:

http://localhost:9870
Step 6: Check the Kafka Connect HDFS connector
Wait about 1–2 minutes for the connector installation.

Run:
curl -s http://localhost:8083/connector-plugins


Look for:
io.confluent.connect.hdfs.HdfsSinkConnector


If it is missing, check the Connect container logs:
docker logs kafka-connect


Step 7: Create the Kafka topic
docker exec kafka kafka-topics --create --topic hdfs-topic --partitions 1 --replication-factor 1 --bootstrap-server kafka:9092


Expected output:
Created topic hdfs-topic.


If the topic already exists, you can continue.
Step 8: Create the HDFS Sink configuration
nano hdfs-sink.json


Paste this JSON:

{
  "name": "hdfs-sink",
  "config": {
    "connector.class": "io.confluent.connect.hdfs.HdfsSinkConnector",
    "tasks.max": "1",
    "topics": "hdfs-topic",
    "hdfs.url": "hdfs://namenode:9000",
    "flush.size": "3",
    "format.class": "io.confluent.connect.hdfs.json.JsonFormat",
    "key.converter": "org.apache.kafka.connect.storage.StringConverter",
    "value.converter": "org.apache.kafka.connect.json.JsonConverter",
    "value.converter.schemas.enable": "false",
    "topics.dir": "/topics",
    "logs.dir": "/logs",
    "confluent.topic.bootstrap.servers": "kafka:9092",
    "confluent.topic.replication.factor": "1"
  }
}



Save using Ctrl + O → Enter → Ctrl + X.
Step 9: Register the connector
Run:
curl -X POST -H "Content-Type: application/json" --data @hdfs-sink.json http://localhost:8083/connectors


Check the status:
curl -s http://localhost:8083/connectors/hdfs-sink/status


Expected result should contain:
"state":"RUNNING"


The task should also show "state":"RUNNING".
Step 10: Send three records to Kafka
Copy and run this entire command:
printf '{"id":1,"name":"Ravi"}\n{"id":2,"name":"Anitha"}\n{"id":3,"name":"Kiran"}\n' | docker exec -i kafka kafka-console-producer --topic hdfs-topic --bootstrap-server kafka:9092


This sends three JSON records to the hdfs-topic topic. The Kafka Connect sink should transfer them to HDFS.
Step 11: Check the files in HDFS
Wait about 10–30 seconds, then execute:
docker exec namenode hdfs dfs -ls -R /topics


You should find a path similar to:
/topics/hdfs-topic/partition=0/


Inside that folder, look for a .json file.
Step 12: Display the stored records
Run this command to read the JSON file directly from HDFS:
docker exec namenode bash -c 'hdfs dfs -cat /topics/hdfs-topic/partition=0/*.json'


Expected output:
{"id":1,"name":"Ravi"}
{"id":2,"name":"Anitha"}
{"id":3,"name":"Kiran"}


This is the main proof of successful execution: the records sent to Kafka have been stored in HDFS.
Step 13: Stop the lab
After confirming the output, delete the connector and stop the containers.
curl -X DELETE http://localhost:8083/connectors/hdfs-sink
docker compose down


Use docker-compose down if that is the command supported by your Ubuntu system.
Result
Kafka was integrated with the Hadoop ecosystem using Kafka Connect and the HDFS Sink connector. Three JSON records were produced to the Kafka topic hdfs-topic and successfully stored in HDFS.
